think The image/README named the next Season G doors; the highest-Lindy crux that is also SLC-small: turn the static caption into a TIMED subtitle track -- multiple cues, each shown only over its frame span. That is the natural completion of text_caption and a genuinely reused video primitive (every player shows subtitles), composing proven verbs with no new format.
think Design: a Cue is a half-open span [start, end) plus text; a track is a bounded ordered disjoint list; validate_track refuses EmptyTrack/TooManyCues/CueEmptySpan/CueBeyondReel/CuesOverlap by name before a pixel; active_cue(track, k) returns the one cue containing k or null. The panel place is fixed (reused text_panel.Panel); only the text changes per cue, so the panel rect depends only on cue text -- opaque by construction.
think Crux proof against the plain no-subtitle background as ground truth: a gap frame equals background byte for byte (no panel where no cue active); a shown frame differs from background only within the panel rect (touches the card only); within a cue span the panel rect is byte-steady frame to frame; two different cues render different panels. Then round-trip byte-for-byte through both codecs (gfv1, gfd1), gfv1 refused at gfd1 magic; delta/keyframe mix REPORTED not claimed (REDS #82).
obs GREEN on the first metal run: 3 cues over 24 frames of 96x48, 17 shown + 7 gap frames, each cue byte-steady and distinct from the next, round-trip byte-identical through both codecs; gfd1 8196 bytes vs gfv1 10271.
obs Reused text_paint.solid rather than reaching for a raw qoi.Pixmap constructor (there is no Pixmap.init); the module composes only public verbs (background verbs, paint_text_panel, both codecs), braiding none.
obs tame_style_check bans clean (no compound asserts, unqualified assert, no memcpy site), width-check exit 0, new prose plain ASCII; every fn carries >=2 positive // invariant asserts.
